INT. PAWNSHOP BACK ROOM, DOWNTOWN - NIGHT

A cramped room of glass cases and hanging guitars. A single desk, a green banker's lamp, a safe in the corner. Rain beads the barred window.

SOLOMON KAPLAN-ADEBAYO (60s, shirtsleeves, suspenders, spectacles on a chain) sits behind the desk counting bills. Across from him, perched on a stool, sits his daughter NADIA KAPLAN (30s, trench coat, wet hair), who has come in from the rain and not yet taken her coat off.

By the door, hat in hands, shifts a young man: ELIJAH OSEI-MARCHETTI (20s, work jacket, wedding ring still new). At the safe, crouched with his back to them all, a fourth figure: UNCLE RUBEN (70s, cardigan, thick glasses), who has been sorting the same papers for ten minutes.

SOLOMON
Nadia, you're dripping on the floor.

NADIA
I came to talk, Papa.

SOLOMON
Talk. I'm listening.

NADIA
Not with him in the room.

Elijah shifts. Solomon rubs his eyes under the spectacles, sets down the bills, and lifts his chin at the door; Elijah sets his hat on a guitar case, then thinks better and picks it up again; Ruben turns from the safe, slow as a tortoise, and glances from face to face, then goes back to the papers. Nadia stands, takes a step toward her father, then retreats a step, and finally unbuttons the coat as if it's a decision she's been postponing for years.

NADIA (CONT'D)
The shop is losing money.

SOLOMON
The shop has always lost money.

NADIA
Not like this.

SOLOMON
Who told you?

NADIA
The books.

SOLOMON
You looked at my books?

NADIA
Papa, I've been doing your books for five years.

SOLOMON
Then you know the shop is losing money.

NADIA
And you've known it's been losing more.

Solomon says nothing. Ruben drops a paper.

ELIJAH
Mr. Kaplan, if I may--

SOLOMON
You may not.

ELIJAH
I'd like to help.

SOLOMON
You'd like to marry my daughter and inherit a debt.

ELIJAH
I already married her.

SOLOMON
Congratulations. Now pick up that hat.

Elijah picks up the hat from the guitar case. Nadia sets her coat on the desk, crosses her arms, and speaks very low.

NADIA
The loan is due Friday.

SOLOMON
What loan?

NADIA
The one you didn't tell me about.

Solomon takes off his spectacles. Folds them. Sets them on the bills.

RUBEN
(from the safe, gently)
Shlomo. Tell her.

SOLOMON
Ruben.

RUBEN
Tell her about the guitar.

SOLOMON
What guitar?

RUBEN
The Gibson. In the back. The one you've been guarding.

Nadia's head swivels to the cases. A sunburst Gibson, hanging half-hidden behind a banjo.

NADIA
What about it?

SOLOMON
It's not mine.

NADIA
Whose is it?

SOLOMON
It belongs to a man who didn't come back for it.

ELIJAH
How long?

SOLOMON
Thirty-one years.

Silence. Rain on the window. Ruben closes the safe with a soft click.

NADIA
(low)
Papa. What's it worth?

SOLOMON
(quietly)
More than the shop.
